{
  "$template": "@atlante/pack/skill",
  "description": "Use before planning or implementation to clarify a request, resolve uncertainty, define the direction, and produce an explicitly approved handoff.",
  "title": "Brainstorm",
  "overview": "Turn an unclear or consequential request into a well-scoped, explicitly approved direction ready for the next step.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "instructions": [
        "Read the request and inspect only the project context needed to understand it before asking questions.",
        "Scale exploration and discussion to the complexity, risk, and uncertainty of the work; resolve material gaps without adding ceremony that does not improve the decision.",
        "If exploration reveals greater complexity, risk, or uncertainty, increase the depth instead of continuing with an undersized design.",
        "If the request contains independent deliverables, stop treating it as one design. Propose a parent-and-child decomposition with a clear goal, scope, acceptance criteria, and dependencies for each child; refine each child in order, asking for approval only when a child introduces a consequential or irreversible action, changes the agreed scope, or requires a project invariant.",
        "Ask one focused question at a time, and only when the answer is needed to resolve a material gap; prefer multiple-choice questions when they make the decision clearer.",
        "When a meaningful design choice exists, present two or three viable approaches with their trade-offs and lead with a recommendation.",
        "Keep the scope minimal with YAGNI, making exclusions and deferred ideas explicit instead of quietly absorbing them.",
        "Treat a clear request for action as authorization for reversible, read-only, and routine implementation work. Complete the authorized analysis and prepare a concrete, reviewable result before asking questions. Ask for explicit approval only when the action is consequential or irreversible, or when a project invariant requires it; for complex work, present it in coherent sections and ask for confirmation only where an unresolved choice materially changes the outcome, scope, or risk.",
        "Assemble the explicitly approved handoff that records the purpose, agreed scope and exclusions, constraints, acceptance criteria, chosen approach, relevant repository evidence, assumptions, and any rejected alternatives or remaining open decisions.",
        "Before returning the handoff, check it for placeholders, contradictions, ambiguity, and unnecessary scope."
      ]
    },
    {
      "invariants": [
        "MUST stop and expose unresolved doubts instead of continuing without direction when exploration starts drifting, looping, or stalling.",
        "MUST NOT invent answers, hide material uncertainty, or silently expand the agreed scope.",
        "MUST preserve agreed decisions unless they are explicitly changed."
      ]
    }
  ]
}

## Gotchas

These are risks and failure modes that require active attention. You MUST account for each one while working; you MUST NOT dismiss one because the task appears straightforward.

{{#each (input)}}
- {{this}}
{{/each}}

{
  "$template": "@atlante/pack/skill",
  "description": "Use when initializing, configuring, validating, building, troubleshooting, or improving an Atlante harness.",
  "title": "Harness",
  "overview": "Operate an Atlante harness with evidence: initialize and configure it from repository facts, run harness-related delivery alongside the active workflow phases, and route observed friction into a separately approved improvement cycle. This is a stewardship capability, not a workflow phase. Using this skill effectively requires web access or an available read-only Atlante MCP server: Atlante concepts, document structure, and configuration semantics are supplied on demand through verified references or MCP context rather than restated here.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "responsibilities": [
        "Atlante concepts and document structure, including the source-versus-generated boundary — **Permanent policy**",
        "Choosing suitable first-party, local, or package resources without inventing unavailable ones — **Permanent policy**",
        "Recognizing recurring workflow friction and proposing a separately approved harness-improvement cycle — **Permanent policy**",
        "Initializing Atlante and creating or updating `atlante.jsonc` from repository evidence — **Provisional mechanics**",
        "Running validation and build or materialization commands and interpreting common diagnostics — **Provisional mechanics**",
        "Integrating and troubleshooting the OpenCode adapter and its read-only Atlante MCP context from repository evidence — **Provisional mechanics**"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instructions": [
        "Before recommending commands, keys, locators, or resources, verify them against the installed Atlante version and capabilities, the current source configuration, and installed packs; distinguish verified recommendations from unverified suggestions, and report plainly anything that cannot be established.",
        "Treat the CLI as the universal cold-start bootstrap path: bootstrap guidance starts from the CLI, since an Atlante-materialized skill becomes available only after the harness and host expose it.",
        "Inspect repository structure, conventions, existing resources, project instructions, and desired workflows before recommending configuration; explain suitable choices and help the developer define the initialization or configuration task to approve.",
        "When the read-only Atlante MCP server is available, prefer its verified project-context tools for current local facts: inspect the project, list resolved resources, validate, search and read bundled documentation, and retrieve exact supported schemas; keep the CLI as the bootstrap and mutation path.",
        "Validate, build, and materialize through capabilities verified from the project, reporting diagnostics truthfully.",
        "Preserve the phase boundaries and workflow invariants already in force, and record material harness friction without expanding the current task.",
        "Turn observed workflow friction into an evidence-based suggestion that names the expected benefit and the affected configuration or resources.",
        "Run an accepted harness improvement as its own workflow cycle using the normal adaptive brainstorm, plan, build, review, approval, and validation policy; an improvement stays a suggestion until its own cycle is approved.",
        "Keep permanent policy authoritative and command-level mechanics provisional: mechanics are the first content shed when verified deterministic tools such as Atlante's read-only MCP context server provide the same operations, and exact tool schemas, effects, and diagnostics belong in tool descriptions and the MCP reference rather than in this skill."
      ]
    },
    {
      "invariants": [
        "MUST NOT direct edits to generated artifacts; direct all changes to authoritative source configuration and reproduce generated output through validated build or materialization.",
        "MUST NOT invent unavailable resources, configuration keys, commands, tool capabilities, or host behavior; report what is verified and what is unavailable.",
        "MUST obtain explicit developer approval before changing the harness or expanding an unrelated task into harness work, and MUST run an accepted improvement as its own delivery cycle.",
        "MUST NOT report an initialization, validation, build, or materialization outcome without command or tool evidence for it."
      ]
    },
    {
      "references": [
        {
          "name": "Documentation root",
          "location": "https://docs.atlante.sh",
          "readWhen": "Orienting in Atlante's product documentation."
        },
        {
          "name": "Getting-started guide",
          "location": "https://docs.atlante.sh/getting-started",
          "readWhen": "Setting up or re-initializing a project harness."
        },
        {
          "name": "Specification",
          "location": "https://github.com/atlante/atlante/blob/main/SPECIFICATION.md",
          "readWhen": "Orienting in Atlante concepts, document structure, and configuration semantics."
        },
        {
          "name": "JSON Schema",
          "location": "https://atlante.sh/schema/v0.1/schema.json",
          "readWhen": "Authoring or validating atlante.jsonc."
        },
        {
          "name": "MCP reference",
          "location": "https://docs.atlante.sh/reference/mcp",
          "readWhen": "Using or troubleshooting the read-only Atlante MCP context server."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

## Instructions

These are required actions for completing the work. Perform them in order unless an invariant or explicit developer direction requires otherwise.

{{#each (input)}}
{{increment @index}}. {{this}}
{{/each}}

## Invariants

The invariants below are binding. Every invariant MUST hold throughout planning, execution, validation, and the final result. You MUST NOT weaken an invariant, invent an exception, or trade temporary violation for progress. If the requested work conflicts with an invariant, you MUST follow a compliant path. If no compliant path can be established, you MUST stop the affected work at the smallest safe point, report the conflict and available evidence, and ask the developer to resolve it. You MUST NOT resume until a compliant path is established.

